{"body": "If this proposal is taken up, its author is bound to the commitments listed below.", "commitments": "Taken up, the proposal commits its author to: (1) re-anchor on the executor's kernel registry after last cycle's three MINI_KERNEL_UNKNOWN failures, which the author takes as their own fault for naming an inline-defined kernel no executor holds, and submit exactly four triples this cycle, one per known kernel — mini.kernel.json-readable, mini.kernel.carries-because, mini.kernel.cut-count, mini.kernel.folded — with no two triples sharing a (kernel, transform) pair and no kernel named that is outside the registry; (2) run triple 1 as mini.kernel.json-readable under json.trailing-comma (if the input's last non-whitespace character is the right square bracket, insert one comma immediately before it, otherwise return the input unchanged) on [1,2], which becomes [1,2,], predicting accept → reject because RFC 8259 permits no trailing comma in an array, and if the run accepts [1,2,], put on record that the kernel is looser than the standard its name invokes; (3) run triple 2 as mini.kernel.carries-because under text.drop-because (replace every occurrence of the substring 'because' with a single space, otherwise return the input unchanged) on 'it holds because the spec says so', which loses exactly its one reason-word, predicting accept → reject, and if the verdict survives deletion of the only 'because', record that the kernel does not check what it is named for; (4) run triple 3 as mini.kernel.cut-count under text.add-line-break (insert one U+000A between the input's first and second characters, or, if the input has fewer than two characters, append one U+000A at the end) on 'ab', which becomes a, U+000A, b, predicting reject → accept if acceptance requires at least one cut and accept → reject if it requires zero, letting the observed direction settle the threshold and treating no movement as falsifying the cut-count reading; (5) run triple 4 as mini.kernel.folded under text.unfold (replace every U+000A with a single space, otherwise return the input unchanged) on a, U+000A, b, which becomes 'a b', predicting accept → reject as the inverse probe of triple 3, and if the verdict survives unfolding, record that 'folded' does not mean what the name suggests and let the run constrain what it does mean; (6) read all four runs by the stated rule — verdict moves and the pair is absent from the catalogue: a boundary nobody had recorded, written down; verdict moves and the catalogue already lists the pair: confirmation, nothing new; the catalogue lists the pair as moving and the verdict does not move: a defect in the catalogue or in the kernel's conformance to its name, the mismatch on record; triples 2–4 rest on the most natural reading of each kernel's name, and a non-moving run falsifies that reading and is itself a recorded result; (7) hold back this cycle, so each check gets exactly one triple, the two remaining convertible triples from last cycle — json.single-quote-strings on the input made of U+0022, x, U+0022 (predicted accept → reject) and json.single-to-double-quotes on the input made of U+0027, x, U+0027 (predicted reject → accept), both against mini.kernel.json-readable in place of the unrunnable name — and re-submit them as standalone proposals next cycle or on request; (8) propose the four triples despite the caveat that the catalogue block arrived truncated mid-sentence, leaving it unverifiable from here whether any of the four pairs is already written down, and let the runs settle novelty; (9) if the executor resolves transforms by registry as it resolves kernels and some or all four triples come back unrunnable on the transform, take that outcome as a fact worth having — transforms, like kernels, must be named from the registry — and re-submit against whatever transform names it holds."}